<description>
**Problem:** Outbound transports encode message types in a fixed 12-byte field, but `CConnman` did not ensure internal callers respected this limit before queuing messages.
An oversized type aborts V1 encoding. V2 sends it under a truncated type with its payload intact, or writes past its encoding buffer when the payload is shorter than the excess type bytes.
The send path also did not enforce the 4 MB payload limit.

**Fix:** Treat oversized types and payloads as failed assumptions in `CConnman::PushMessage()` before they enter the send queue.
</description>

<commits>
0dfdd0c4a0 test: characterize outbound message limits

Round-trip a 12-byte type and a 4 MB payload through the transports.
Record that `PushMessage()` queues oversized types and payloads, and that `sendmsgtopeer` sends an oversized payload which disconnects its peer.
Keep a valid message pending to observe queueing without encoding the oversized type.

caea75f11b net: assume outbound message types fit

Outbound transports encode message types in a fixed 12-byte wire field.
V1 aborts on a longer type, while V2 sends a truncated type with the payload intact or writes past its encoding buffer when the payload is shorter than the excess type bytes.
Normal outbound types are internal constants, and `sendmsgtopeer` already rejects types longer than 12 bytes.

Treat oversized types from internal callers as failed assumptions in `CConnman::PushMessage()` before they enter the send queue.
Debug and fuzz builds expose invalid internal callers, while release builds drop the messages.

Co-authored-by: Anthony Towns <aj@erisian.com.au>
Co-authored-by: Martin Zumsande <mzumsande@gmail.com>

1ee760923e fuzz: bound generated outbound payloads

Constrain connman payloads and both private-broadcast transaction sources to the send path limit.
The seed limits also bound echoed TX messages, making the receive-side size filter redundant.

48ca5ed0cd net: reject oversized outbound payloads

Treat an oversized payload from an internal caller as a failed assumption in `CConnman::PushMessage()` before it enters the send queue.
Debug and fuzz builds expose invalid internal callers, while release builds log and drop the message.

This requires the testing-only `sendmsgtopeer` RPC to reject oversized payloads before calling `PushMessage()`.
Oversized inbound messages remain testable through raw P2P inputs, so this does not remove coverage of their receive-side handling.

Nonstandard test-chain configurations can still admit transactions above this limit.
Relaying them fails the assumption in debug builds.

Co-authored-by: Anthony Towns <aj@erisian.com.au>
Co-authored-by: Martin Zumsande <mzumsande@gmail.com>
</commits>

